            <ArticleTitle>Previous Injury and Chronic Pain are Associated with Side of Onset in Parkinson's Disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.16966/2379-7150.109</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Parkinson's disease (PD) motor symptoms are frequently asymmetric and the factors that influence the side of onset are unclear.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To explore whether peripheral injury and associated chronic limb pain may influence the side of onset.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We administered a questionnaire to 128 PD patients in a tertiary movement disorder clinic. Handedness, date and type of limb injury(s) and duration of associated pain, and date and side of onset were ascertained.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Sixty-two subjects reported limb injuries prior to the onset of PD symptoms, 30 with and 32 without chronic pain (i.e., ≥ 2 months). There was no association between injury and PD onset side overall (p=0.334). In subjects with chronic pain associated with limb injuries, however, side of injuries was associated with the side of PD symptom onset (p=0.030).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Limb injury with chronic pain may be related to the side of PD symptom onset. Future studies may shed light on the nature of this observation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
